# Can a program tell whether any program will finish? Turing's halting problem
tags: ["halting problem","alan turing","computability","undecidability","busy beaver"]
teaches: ["concepts/halting-problem","methods/diagonal-argument","concepts/rices-theorem"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed","b":"gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace","take":"lines"}
prompt: "Why would a perfect bug-checker contradict itself when run on itself? What does this mean for real software and real computers?"
next: {"title":"Can a surprise be announced? The surprise exam paradox","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/paradoxes/08-can-a-surprise-be-announced-the-surprise.cast"}

## Turing's halting problem
sources:
    - id: turing1936
      title: On Computable Numbers, with an Application to the Entscheidungsproblem
      authors: Alan M. Turing
      year: 1936
      finding: No general method can decide, for every machine, whether it ever finishes its computation; the proof applies the diagonal process (section 8). Received 28 May 1936; Proc. London Math. Soc. (2) 42.
    - id: barina2025
      title: Improved verification limit for the convergence of the Collatz conjecture
      authors: David Barina
      year: 2025
      finding: Every starting number below 2^71 (about 2.36 × 10^21) reaches 1 (Journal of Supercomputing).
    - id: rice1953
      title: Classes of recursively enumerable sets and their decision problems
      authors: H. G. Rice
      year: 1953
      finding: 'Every non-trivial property of what a program computes is undecidable (Trans. AMS 74: 358–366).'
    - id: cohen1987
      title: 'Computer viruses: theory and experiments'
      authors: Fred Cohen
      year: 1987
      finding: 'Perfect detection of computer viruses is undecidable (Computers & Security 6: 22–35).'
    - id: bb5
      title: Determination of the fifth Busy Beaver value
      authors: The bbchallenge Collaboration
      year: 2025
      finding: The longest-running halting 5-state, 2-symbol Turing machine stops after 47,176,870 steps; proof announced July 2024 and verified in Coq (arXiv 2509.12337).
    - id: antihydra
      title: Antihydra (bbchallenge wiki)
      authors: bbchallenge
      year: 2024
      finding: A 6-state Turing machine whose halting depends on an open Collatz-like problem; believed never to halt, unproved.

