    card title "Will it ever stop?"

A: Here's a question every p (@draw p_count@)rogrammer has met: can one program look at any other program and tell whether it will ever finish?

B: For most programs you can just t (@draw t_halts@)ell. That one counts down to zer (@draw p_hello@)o and stops. That one prints hel (@draw t_loops@)lo forever.

    erase p_count t_halts p_hello t_loops

A: Then try this one. Take a number. I (@draw r_even@)f it's even, halve it; i (@draw r_odd@)f it's odd, triple it and add one. Stop w (@draw r_stop@)hen you reach one.

A: Start at  (@draw run6@)six: three, ten, five, sixteen, eight, four, two, one. Eight steps,  (@draw run6_n@)and it stops.

A: Now start at twenty-seven.
    draw sc27

    ask "Starting at 27, how high does the number climb before it falls back to 1? Click on the line." on sc27 store g default "1000" voice b
        right: Close: you said {g}, and it peaks at {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; it climbs all the way to {g.true}.

A: Nine thousa (@draw run27@)nd two hundred and thirty-two. And it takes a hundred and eleve (@draw run27_n@)n steps to get back down to one.

    erase sc27

B: Wild, but it did stop. Does it  (@draw every@)stop for every starting number?

A: Nobody knows. Computers have checked every start (@draw checked@) up to twenty-one digits long, and they all stop, but no one has pro (@draw proof@)ved they always will.

    erase run6 run6_n run27 run27_n every checked proof

B: Then just run it and wait.
    draw w_line w_run

A: Waiting only tells you one thing. If it stops, you know. If it' (@draw w_y@)s still running after a year, it might stop tomorrow, (@draw w_q@) or never.

    erase rules w_line w_run w_y w_q

A: What we want is a checker (@draw h_in h_a0 h_box@). Call it H: hand it any program and its input, and it ans (@draw h_a1 h_halts h_a2 h_loops@)wers halts or loops, always,  (@draw h_fin@)in finite time.

A: And in 1936, a twenty-three-year-o (@draw turing turing_y@)ld Cambridge mathematician, Alan Turing, proved that no such checker can exist.

B: Proved it? Not "nobody's managed it yet", but "it can't be done"?
    highlight h_box

    erase turing turing_y

A: It can't be done. Our route: suppose H exists, build a troublemaker out of it, feed the troublemaker to itself, and watch H tie itself in a knot.
    blocking false

    flow h_a0 h_a1 h_a2 duration 3

    highlight h_box effect circle duration 3

    clear

    card title "The troublemaker"

B: Go on, then. Suppose someone hands us H.
    draw d_h

A: Programs are just text, so a program can be the input of another program, even of itself.
    draw d_p d_pa d_pa_l

A: Now build D ar (@draw d_box d_lab@)ound it. D takes a program P and asks  (@highlight d_pa d_h duration 2.5@)H: does P halt when it's fed its own text?

A: Then D does the opposite. If H sa (@draw d_a1 d_a1_l d_loop@)ys halts, D loops forever. If H  (@draw d_a2 d_a2_l d_stop@)says loops, D stops at once.

B: Petty. But every part of D is ordinary code, apart from H.
    highlight d_box d_loop d_stop

    erase d_p d_pa_l

A: Exactly. Now the one move tha (@draw d_self d_selfp d_selfa d_self_l@)t matters: feed D its own text. Does D, run on D, halt?

A: Suppose H answers halts.
    highlight d_a1 d_a1_l

    ask "H says D halts. Which way does D go? Tap it." choose ["d_loop","d_stop"] answer d_loop store way voice b
        right: Into the loop: D always does the opposite of H's answer.
        wrong: D does the opposite of whatever H says.

A: Into the loop. So D runs for (@draw d_w1@)ever, and H was wrong.

B: Then H must say loops.
    highlight d_a2 d_a2_l

A: Then D stops at once, and  (@draw d_w2@)H is wrong again.

B: Every answer it can give is wrong.
    highlight d_w1 d_w2

A: And every step except H was ordinary programming. So the broken step is the on (@draw x_h@)e we assumed: a perfect halting checker can't exist.

B: It's the liar's trick, in code: a program that does the opposite of whatever you predict about it.
    highlight d_box effect box color #8a5fa8

    clear

    card title "The diagonal again"

A: There's a second way to see it, and it's Cantor's diagonal from the hotel lecture. (@draw tb_frame@) List every program down the side, and the same programs as inputs along the top.

A: Each c (@draw cells@)ell is H's answer: does this program halt on that input?

A: (@draw diag@) D reads only the diagonal, each program run on its own tex (@draw tb_dr tb_d drow@)t, and flips it: halts becomes loops, loops becomes halts.

B: So D differs from the first program in the first cell, from the second in the second…
    blocking false

    highlight c11 dd1 duration 2

    highlight c22 dd2 duration 2

A: …and from every program somewhere. So D is nowhere in the list. Bu (@draw tb_not@)t D is a program, and the list had them all.
    highlight c33 dd3 c44 dd4 duration 2.2

A: Same knot, drawn as a table. Turing took the trick from C (@draw tb_cantor@)antor; his paper calls it the diagonal process.
    highlight diag drow duration 3

    clear

    card title "What a computer can't know"

B: Lovely. But does any of this touch t (@draw q_crash@)he software on my phone?

A: More than you'd think. In  (@draw rice@)1953 Henry Rice showed that any question about what a program does, like will it crash (@draw q_leak q_virus@), leak a password or act like a virus, is as hard as halting.

A: So real checkers, from compilers (@draw ck ck_a1 ck_safe ck_a2 ck_unsafe@) to virus scanners, settle for three answers: safe, unsafe, (@draw ck_a3 ck_dk@) and don't know.

    erase qs rice checker

A: It even limits what we can know about tiny program (@draw bb@)s. Take every machine of Turing's simplest kind with five states, and ask which of the ones that stop runs longest.

    ask "How many steps does the longest-running five-state machine take before it stops? Click on the line." on bb store s default "10000" gap false voice b
        right: Close: you said {s}; the record is {s.true}.
        wrong: You said {s}; the record is {s.true}.

A: Forty-sev (@draw bb_n@)en million, one hundred and seventy-six thousand, eight hundred and seventy steps. Proving that no five- (@draw bb_y@)state machine stops later took until 2024, and an online team of volunteers.

A: With six states i (@draw bb6@)t's out of reach: one six-state machine stops only if a sequence much like our (@draw bb_ghost@) twenty-seven one behaves a certai (@highlight bb6 effect circle duration 2.5@)n way, and nobody can tell whether it will.

B: My laptop  (@draw laptop@)isn't infinite, though. With finite memo (@erase bb_ghost@)ry it must stop or repeat itself sooner or later.

A: True, so in principle it can be checked. But  (@draw kb@)one kilobyte of memory already has about ten to the power two thousand four hundred and sixty-six states.

    erase bb bb_n bb_y bb6 laptop kb

A: So here's what Turing (@draw checker@) found: any checker that claims to predict every program can be (@highlight ck duration 2.5@) handed a program built to do the opposite. Perfect prediction is impossible, and honest tools (@highlight ck_dk duration 3@) must sometimes say don't know.

    erase checker

    quiz "A company says its new tool can check any program, on any input, however long it runs, and always tell correctly whether it will ever stop. What should you think?" voice b
        right: It can't be perfect: a program could ask the tool about itself and do the opposite, as D did. A tool that sometimes says 'unsure' is honest, but that isn't what it claims.
        wrong: Think of the troublemaker: what could someone build with that tool inside it?
        * OK if it says 'unsure'
        + It can't be perfect
        * Fine if it's well tested

    clear

    card title "Will it ever stop?"

B: So, can (@draw rules end_every@) a program tell whether any program will finish? Not every one. And Turin (@draw end_h x_endh@)g proved it before anyone had built a modern computer.

## Where next
