    '|a||Now build D around it. D takes a program P and asks H: does P halt when it''s fed its own text?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8480
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1737gv'
    '|a||Then D does the opposite. If H says halts, D loops forever. If H says loops, D stops at once.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7200
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:183kg2u'
    '|b||Petty. But every part of D is ordinary code, apart from H.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5960
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:1tt0aiy'
    '|a||Exactly. Now the one move that matters: feed D its own text. Does D, run on D, halt?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8880
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1pv6t75'
    '|a||Suppose H answers halts.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2000
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:cs17jp'
    '|b||H says D halts. Which way does D go? Tap it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4080
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:n5ykw6'
    '|b||Into the loop: D always does the opposite of H''s answer.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4120
      voice: gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace
    '|a||Into the loop. So D runs forever, and H was wrong.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4560
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|b||Then H must say loops.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2320
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:tsiy3p'
    '|a||Then D stops at once, and H is wrong again.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4280
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1e74x5s'
    '|b||Every answer it can give is wrong.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 1960
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:1r7020q'
    '|a||And every step except H was ordinary programming. So the broken step is the one we assumed: a perfect halting checker can''t exist.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9880
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1qfkjmw'
    '|b||It''s the liar''s trick, in code: a program that does the opposite of whatever you predict about it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6040
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:tzzjku'
    '|a||There''s a second way to see it, and it''s Cantor''s diagonal from the hotel lecture. List every program down the side, and the same programs as inputs along the top.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9720
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:eklupr'
    '|a||Each cell is H''s answer: does this program halt on that input?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4360
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:95jnjk'
    '|a||D reads only the diagonal, each program run on its own text, and flips it: halts becomes loops, loops becomes halts.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7800
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1bq9doc'
    '|b||So D differs from the first program in the first cell, from the second in the second…':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5640
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:ceom57'
    '|a||…and from every program somewhere. So D is nowhere in the list. But D is a program, and the list had them all.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9400
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1spcl69'
    '|a||Same knot, drawn as a table. Turing took the trick from Cantor; his paper calls it the diagonal process.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6800
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1glm31x'
    '|b||Lovely. But does any of this touch the software on my phone?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3880
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:1fuj47a'
    '|a||More than you''d think. In 1953 Henry Rice showed that any question about what a program does, like will it crash, leak a password or act like a virus, is as hard as halting.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11560
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:rjxz67'
    '|a||So real checkers, from compilers to virus scanners, settle for three answers: safe, unsafe, and don''t know.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9200
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:st4ew0'
    '|a||It even limits what we can know about tiny programs. Take every machine of Turing''s simplest kind with five states, and ask which of the ones that stop runs longest.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11080
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1vh3usp'
    '|b||How many steps does the longest-running five-state machine take before it stops? Click on the line.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6480
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:16ul70r'
    '|a||Forty-seven million, one hundred and seventy-six thousand, eight hundred and seventy steps. Proving that no five-state machine stops later took until 2024, and an online team of volunteers.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 13200
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||With six states it''s out of reach: one six-state machine stops only if a sequence much like our twenty-seven one behaves a certain way, and nobody can tell whether it will.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11640
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1l2ixi3'
    '|b||My laptop isn''t infinite, though. With finite memory it must stop or repeat itself sooner or later.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6960
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:4o9410'
    '|a||True, so in principle it can be checked. But one kilobyte of memory already has about ten to the power two thousand four hundred and sixty-six states.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10760
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:10x1bcc'
    '|a||So here''s what Turing found: any checker that claims to predict every program can be handed a program built to do the opposite. Perfect prediction is impossible, and honest tools must sometimes say don''t know.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 14000
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1r5xx6w'
    '|b||A company says its new tool can check any program, on any input, however long it runs, and always tell correctly whether it will ever stop. What should you think?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11880
      voice: 'gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace #ctx:1uwpm28'
    '|b||It can''t be perfect: a program could ask the tool about itself and do the opposite, as D did. A tool that sometimes says ''unsure'' is honest, but that isn''t what it claims.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9600
      voice: gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace
    '|b||So, can a program tell whether any program will finish? Not every one. And Turing proved it before anyone had built a modern computer.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8320
      voice: gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace
    '|a||Next: Can a surprise be announced? The surprise exam paradox':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5200
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|b||D does the opposite of whatever H says.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3240
      voice: gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace
    '|b||Think of the troublemaker: what could someone build with that tool inside it?':
